sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch06-16> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Systems that intrinsically generate randomness, like rule 22 or rule 30, produce the same overall random behavior with the same large-scale properties whatever their initial conditions, giving them a fundamental stability that he says underlies the apparent stability of many systems in nature.";
    dc:type "general-principle";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Intrinsic randomness generation makes large-scale behavior stable";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 6, “Starting from Randomness”; section “Special Initial Conditions” (from p. 266), p. 266. Scope: intrinsically random cellular automata and natural systems with apparent stability"
}
